gh grants or tax incentives for companies adopting AI-based upskilling programs, rather than attempting to slow the inevitable tide of progress.\n\nThe fundamental challenge lies in the fact that governments are inherently unprepared for the speed and scale of AI-driven workforce shifts, making true preparedness contingent on breaking down outdated structures and adopting adaptive governance. Existing institutional inertia, characterized by slow legislative cycles and bureaucratic processes, creates a temporal mismatch in accountability bootstrapping, meaning traditional frameworks simply cannot keep pace with technological evolution. We cannot expect a 20th-century regulatory apparatus to manage 21st-century technological transformation effectively. Instead, policymakers must embrace agile regulatory sandboxes, similar to those successfully implemented in fintech, where AI innovators can test new applications with temporary waivers from certain existing regulations, as outlined in Executive Order 13960 on Promoting the Use of Trustworthy AI in the Federal Government. This approach fosters innovation while allowing real-world data to inform future, more adaptive policies, preventing the costly stagnation that comes from over-regulation based on hypothetical risks.\n\nOur position rests on two critical assumptions. First, we assume AI's inherently positive economic and societal impact will be pervasive, creating net benefits across society rather than concentrating wealth and opportunity among a select few; if AI's benefits prove to be narrowly distributed, our approach would need to integrate more aggressive wealth redistribution mechanisms, which we currently believe unnecessary given AI's broad potential. Second, we assume the scalability and effectiveness of AI-assisted learning will be sufficient to re-skill a significant portion of the displaced workforce rapidly, preventing prolonged periods of unemployment; should AI-driven upskilling prove less effective or 