to irreversible societal damage. For example, the rapid deployment of autonomous AI in critical infrastructure, such as energy grids or transportation networks, without stringent, verifiable pre-market safety protocols, could lead to cascading failures that far exceed economic disruption, impacting public safety and national security. This situation underscores the urgency for robust \"International and Institutional AI Governance.\"\n\nTo effectively manage these risks, policymakers must prioritize comprehensive, precautionary regulation, similar to the tiered risk approach proposed by the European Union's AI Act, which mandates stringent assessments for high-risk AI applications before market entry. The U.S. Executive Order 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI Development also acknowledges the need for safety standards, but its implementation requires strong enforcement mechanisms and sufficient funding to ensure compliance. Governments must move beyond reactive measures to establish robust oversight, including independent auditing of AI models, clear liability frameworks for AI-induced harms, and international agreements to prevent a race-to-the-bottom on safety standards. Such proactive measures are not impediments to progress but indispensable prerequisites for any sustainable, beneficial AI integration into society. Without these safeguards, any perceived economic growth will be built on a foundation of increasing fragility and unacceptable risk.",
